<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kollam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Quilon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kollam, formerly Quilon, is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was a major centre of the medieval spice trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, and the Malayalam calendar, the Kollam era, begins in 825 CE. The Tharisapalli <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates of 849 granted privileges to its Syrian Christian church, and it later became a seat of the rulers of Venad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kollam.jpg" alt="Kollam" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arunvrparavur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_India_Kollam_EN.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
